=== task: mcq medium
title: A dense little graph
prompt: A board game has 20 squares, and almost every pair of squares is joined. The game asks "are these two squares joined?" thousands of times a second. Which storage fits best?
* An adjacency matrix :: Right. 400 boxes is tiny, most of them are 1s anyway, and each question is one look-up.
- An adjacency list :: It works, but every question scans a list of up to 19 neighbours, and it saves almost no space on a dense graph.
- An edge list :: Each question could scan about 190 edges.
- None of them can store a graph this dense :: All three can; they differ in speed and space.
hint: How many boxes would a matrix need for 20 squares?
hint: Which storage answers "joined?" in one step?
right: A matrix: for a small, dense graph its V² boxes cost little, and its O(1) look-ups win.
wrong: With only 20 vertices, a matrix needs 400 boxes, and nearly all of them are 1s. It answers each question in O(1), while the lists have to be scanned.

