=== task: multi hard
title: Pick the storage
prompt: Choose **every** pairing of situation and storage that is a good fit.
* A social network with 2 billion users: adjacency lists :: Right. Each user has a few hundred friends at most: very sparse.
* A 30-city map where every pair of cities has a flight: an adjacency matrix :: Right. 900 boxes, nearly all used, with O(1) look-ups.
* Reading a graph from a file of "u v" lines: an edge list :: Right. That's how the data arrives, before converting it.
* Breadth-first search over a big sparse graph: adjacency lists :: Right. O(V + E) instead of O(V²).
- A web crawl with a billion pages: an adjacency matrix :: That's 10¹⁸ boxes, almost all 0.
- Answering "who are u's neighbours?" in a sparse graph: an adjacency matrix :: The matrix has to scan a whole row: O(V) instead of O(degree).
hint: Is the graph sparse or dense?
hint: Which questions does the program ask most?
right: Sparse graphs and neighbour-heavy algorithms suit adjacency lists; small dense graphs suit a matrix; edge lists suit input and output.
wrong: Use a matrix only when V² boxes are affordable and mostly used, and edge checks dominate. Huge sparse graphs need adjacency lists, and edge lists are the natural format for reading graphs in.

