n = len(m)
t = [[0] * n for k in range(n)]
for i in range(n):
    for j in range(n):
        t[j][i] = m[i][j]
print(t)
```
distractors:
```python
t = m
        t[i][j] = m[i][j]
```
hint: Make a fresh n × n matrix for the result.
hint: The box in row i, column j of `m` goes to row j, column i of `t`.
right: Every edge i → j becomes j → i: the transpose of the matrix is the matrix of the reversed graph. It takes O(V²).
wrong: Build a new zero matrix, then copy each `m[i][j]` into `t[j][i]`.
